	<h1>Uses</h1>
	<p class="intro">
		The machines and software I actually use, in the spirit of
		<a href="https://uses.tech" target="_blank" rel="noopener">uses.tech</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Desktop</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>CPU:</strong> Intel Core i9-9900K, a 2018 chip that still refuses to be the bottleneck</li>
		<li><strong>GPU:</strong> NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060</li>
		<li><strong>RAM / board:</strong> 32&nbsp;GB on a Gigabyte Z390 D</li>
		<li><strong>OS:</strong> CachyOS Linux</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Laptop</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Machine:</strong> ThinkPad P1 Gen 7 (RTX 4070, 32&nbsp;GB RAM, 2&times;1&nbsp;TB NVMe)</li>
		<li><strong>OS:</strong> dual boot Fedora + Windows, one drive each, no arguments</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Software</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Editor:</strong> Neovim, with dotfiles I tinker with more than I probably should</li>
		<li><strong>Terminal:</strong> kitty, running fish</li>
		<li><strong>Desktop:</strong> KDE Plasma or Hyprland, depending on the mood</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Server</h2>
	<p>
		A Hetzner box that keeps me honest about deployment, backups, and what "it works on my machine"
		really means:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Nextcloud</strong>: files and sync</li>
		<li><strong>Navidrome</strong>: music streaming</li>
		<li><strong>BookLore</strong>: the library behind the <a href="/log">reading log</a></li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Retro Pi</h2>
	<p>
		A Raspberry Pi turned into a retro gaming box, the other kind of self-hosting.
	</p>

